Iterable interface is the root interface for the collection frame work.
1. If a class implements this iterable interface, then the objects of this class can be iterated using iterator or enhanced for loop.
2. Iterable interface consists of three methods as shown below:

public interface Iterable<T> {
    Iterator<T> iterator();                             // abstract method
    default void forEach(Consumer<? super T> action);   // Java 8
    default Spliterator<T> spliterator();               // Java 8
}

3. iterator() → abstract
4. forEach(Consumer) → default
5. spliterator() → default

-> In order to ensure that the java needs a standard way to iterate the different java collections.

Iterable -> Gives iterator
Iterator -> provides the actual traversAL.

Enhance for loop -> for(DataType var: collection) uses iterator internally. Compiler converts the enhanced for loop into the iterator fashion internally.

list.forEach(x->System.out.println(x)) -> It is used for iteration in the functional way.

