Spliterator
-----------
Spliterator works like an Iterator, but smarter
It can:
1. Traverse elements
2. Split itself into two smaller Spliterators
3. Provide size estimates
4. Provide characteristics that help the Stream optimize work.

Core methods of Spliterator:
----------------------------

boolean tryAdvance(Consumer<? super T> action);
void forEachRemaining(Consumer<? super T> action);

Spliterator<T> trySplit();
long estimateSize();
int characteristics();

tryAdvance() Internal working :
-------------------------------
Moves to the next element
Executes the action on that element
Returns true if an element was processed
Returns false when iteration is complete

forEachRemaining(Consumer):
----------------------------
Loops internally using tryAdvance()
Processes all remaining elements
Optimized for performance

 trySplit() — The Heart of Spliterator
 =======================================
 Internal logic:

Take the current range of elements
Split it into two subranges (almost equal halves)
Return a new Spliterator for the first half
Keep the second half for itself
If splitting is not possible → return null
This is how parallel stream divide the stream of data and makes use of the multiple threads. 


estimateSize() 
Returns remaining elements count.
Useful for:

Load balancing
Optimizing splitting
Predicting work size


characteristics()




