Fails-fast iterator:
-------------------
Fail‑fast iterator is an iterator that throws a ConcurrentModificationException if a collection is structurally modified while it is being iterated (except through the iterator’s own remove() method).


Java collections internally maintain a variable called modCount (modification count).
This count is incremented whenever there is a structural modification, such as adding or removing elements from the collection.


When an iterator is created, it takes a snapshot of the current modCount and stores it in a variable called expectedModCount.


During each iteration step (next(), hasNext()), the iterator compares expectedModCount with the collection’s modCount.

If both values are equal → iteration continues normally.
If they differ → it means the collection was modified during iteration, and the iterator fails fast by throwing a ConcurrentModificationException.



Fail‑fast iterators are mainly designed to detect bugs early and prevent unpredictable behavior, especially in single‑threaded environments where accidental modification during iteration is a common mistake.


Fail-Safe Iterator:
---------------
Fail safe iterator is the iterator which does not throw any exception even though if we do any structural changes while iterating.

-> Fail safe iterators will be using the copy or snapshot of the collections which is taken at the time of iterator object creation.
-> If at all if any structural changes made to the original collections, These changes are not affected to the snapshot or copy of the original collection.
-> These fail safe iterators are mainly used for safety instead of performance.
--> For example, Collections like concurrenthashmap, copyonwriteArraylist uses the fail safe iterators.


